1
00:00:01,530 --> 00:00:03,070
November 1954.

2
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:05,520
A fighter is hanging in the sky.

3
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:08,230
Nose pointed straight up.

4
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:09,830
Propellers roaring.

5
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:11,650
And it's coming down…

6
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:13,500
tail first.

7
00:00:13,780 --> 00:00:15,850
The pilot can't see the
ground ahead of him.

8
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:17,320
It's behind him.

9
00:00:17,660 --> 00:00:22,801
So he twists round in his seat, looks back
over his shoulder, and lowers more than

10
00:00:22,801 --> 00:00:26,250
five tonnes of fighter toward
the earth, mostly by eye.

11
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:30,320
Come down too fast, and
it slams onto its tail.

12
00:00:30,810 --> 00:00:31,850
Drift, and it can tip over.

13
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:36,700
His rival at Lockheed built
a plane to do exactly this.

14
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:38,850
It never once managed it.

15
00:00:39,490 --> 00:00:40,530
This one did.

16
00:00:41,910 --> 00:00:44,610
This is the Convair XFY-1 Pogo.

17
00:00:48,170 --> 00:00:52,140
So why would anyone build a
fighter you have to land backwards?

18
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:56,020
And if it actually worked, why
has almost nobody heard of it?

19
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:58,730
The answer comes down to one problem.

20
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:03,700
And by the end of this video, you'll
see exactly why it helped kill the Pogo.

21
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:05,690
It starts at sea.

22
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:11,257
During the Second World War, Allied convoys
crossing the Atlantic faced attacks from

23
00:01:11,257 --> 00:01:12,910
German U-boats and aircraft.

24
00:01:13,410 --> 00:01:17,994
Protecting those ships meant getting
fighters over the ocean—but conventional

25
00:01:17,994 --> 00:01:22,578
fighters needed runways or aircraft
carriers, and carriers were expensive and

26
00:01:22,578 --> 00:01:23,650
limited in number.

27
00:01:24,530 --> 00:01:27,430
After the war, the Navy
asked a radical question.

28
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:30,700
What if any ship could
carry its own fighter?

29
00:01:31,330 --> 00:01:32,250
No runway.

30
00:01:32,290 --> 00:01:33,120
No catapult.

31
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:34,160
No carrier.

32
00:01:34,330 --> 00:01:36,370
Just a small platform on the deck.

33
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:41,717
After years of study, the Navy wanted a
fighter that could take off straight up from

34
00:01:41,717 --> 00:01:44,000
a ship and land straight back down on it.

35
00:01:44,610 --> 00:01:47,330
In 1951, it ordered prototypes
from Convair and Lockheed.

36
00:01:49,210 --> 00:01:52,160
And both companies came up
with the same basic idea.

37
00:01:52,710 --> 00:01:54,720
A fighter that sits on its tail.

38
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:57,890
And here's the catch
nobody could design away.

39
00:01:58,370 --> 00:02:03,200
If a plane takes off pointing at the
sky, it has to land pointing at the sky.

40
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:06,400
The pilot sits in the
nose, at the very top.

41
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:10,750
So when he comes down to land,
the ground is behind his back.

42
00:02:11,510 --> 00:02:12,510
Keep that in mind.

43
00:02:12,630 --> 00:02:13,840
It's the whole story.

44
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:17,290
Convair's answer looked like
nothing else in the sky.

45
00:02:17,770 --> 00:02:19,570
A short, fat fuselage.

46
00:02:19,930 --> 00:02:20,930
A delta wing.

47
00:02:21,010 --> 00:02:26,060
And a huge tail fin above and below,
so from behind it looked like a cross.

48
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:28,540
It had no conventional landing gear.

49
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:31,950
Just four small wheels, on the
tips of the wings and the fins.

50
00:02:33,040 --> 00:02:34,470
When it was parked, it didn't sit.

51
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:36,400
It stood.

52
00:02:36,760 --> 00:02:43,878
In the nose was an Allison T40, really
two turbine engines driving one gearbox,

53
00:02:43,878 --> 00:02:45,950
about 5,850 horsepower.

54
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:51,829
It turned two propellers, each with three
blades, sixteen feet across, about five

55
00:02:51,829 --> 00:02:54,490
metres, spinning in opposite directions.

56
00:02:55,020 --> 00:02:56,580
Here's how it hung in the air.

57
00:02:56,650 --> 00:03:01,290
To hover, the propellers have to push upward
harder than the whole aircraft weighs.

58
00:03:02,490 --> 00:03:07,440
At maximum takeoff weight, about sixteen
thousand pounds, that's over seven tonnes.

59
00:03:08,650 --> 00:03:10,950
More thrust than weight, it climbs.

60
00:03:11,330 --> 00:03:12,890
Less, and it sinks.

61
00:03:13,410 --> 00:03:16,110
The throttle controls
the climb and the sink.

62
00:03:16,650 --> 00:03:21,620
But the pilot still has to balance it on
its tail with the controls, every second.

63
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:23,790
And there's a second problem.

64
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:28,940
Spin one huge propeller one way, and the
airframe wants to spin the other way.

65
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:30,760
That's Newton's third law.

66
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:32,340
So the Pogo has two.

67
00:03:33,270 --> 00:03:38,320
One turns clockwise, one turns
anticlockwise, and their twist cancels out.

68
00:03:38,850 --> 00:03:41,960
Without that, it would just
spin on its tail like a top.

69
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:46,530
Getting it into the air turned
out to be the easy part.

70
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:50,460
Getting it back down is where
this story gets frightening.

71
00:03:51,140 --> 00:03:56,522
In the spring of 1954, the Pogo was taken
to Moffett Field in California, inside

72
00:03:56,522 --> 00:04:01,030
Hangar One, a building so big it
was built to house a Navy airship.

73
00:04:02,810 --> 00:04:06,920
For safety, it flew tied to
cables, hovering under the roof.

74
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:11,980
Over and over, it rose, hung
there, and came back down.

75
00:04:12,610 --> 00:04:16,020
The man flying it was Convair's
test pilot, James "Skeets" Coleman.

76
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:19,530
Just getting in was strange.

77
00:04:20,930 --> 00:04:25,126
He climbed a ladder to a cockpit two storeys
up, and settled in almost flat on his

78
00:04:25,126 --> 00:04:27,070
back, looking straight at the ceiling.

79
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:32,456
For vertical flight, his seat tilted about
45 degrees toward the instrument panel,

80
00:04:32,456 --> 00:04:35,800
but landing still left him
struggling to see the ground.

81
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:40,500
On the first of August,
1954, the cables came off.

82
00:04:40,990 --> 00:04:44,980
The Pogo took off outside,
free, for the first time.

83
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:46,820
Taking off was simple.

84
00:04:47,630 --> 00:04:49,910
Point it up, open the throttle, and climb.

85
00:04:50,470 --> 00:04:54,915
Once it was high enough, the pilot pushed
the nose over, and it flew like a normal

86
00:04:54,915 --> 00:04:55,240
plane.

87
00:04:55,810 --> 00:04:57,300
Landing was the problem.

88
00:04:57,770 --> 00:05:02,683
He had to slow down, pull the nose up until
the whole aircraft was pointing at the

89
00:05:02,683 --> 00:05:04,960
sky, and hang there on the propellers.

90
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:08,880
Then he had to come down tail-first.

91
00:05:09,480 --> 00:05:11,630
Now look at what he could actually see.

92
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:13,850
In front of him, sky.

93
00:05:14,340 --> 00:05:16,450
Below him, his own aircraft.

94
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:21,550
The only way to see the ground was to twist
round and look back over his shoulder.

95
00:05:22,010 --> 00:05:25,000
From there, he had to
judge three things at once.

96
00:05:25,470 --> 00:05:26,470
How high am I?

97
00:05:26,820 --> 00:05:28,140
How fast am I sinking?

98
00:05:28,490 --> 00:05:30,370
And am I drifting sideways?

99
00:05:30,990 --> 00:05:35,380
A radar altimeter in the wingtip drove
three warning lights to help Coleman judge

100
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:36,030
his descent.

101
00:05:36,510 --> 00:05:38,830
But the lights couldn't
do the landing for him.

102
00:05:39,330 --> 00:05:44,080
Come down too fast, and the impact could
damage the aircraft or make it tip over.

103
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:48,600
Drift sideways, and the narrow landing
stance left little room for error.

104
00:05:49,070 --> 00:05:53,310
And all the time, any gust of wind is
pushing on that big wing and those tall fins.

105
00:05:54,930 --> 00:06:00,576
He's doing this backwards, mostly by eye,
in a fighter that weighs as much as four

106
00:06:00,576 --> 00:06:00,920
cars.

107
00:06:01,450 --> 00:06:05,850
On the second of November, 1954,
Coleman did the whole thing.

108
00:06:06,290 --> 00:06:11,916
Straight up from the ground, over into
level flight, back up to vertical, and down,

109
00:06:11,916 --> 00:06:13,610
backwards, onto its tail.

110
00:06:14,120 --> 00:06:17,020
No aircraft like it had
ever done that before.

111
00:06:17,530 --> 00:06:19,200
It earned him the Harmon Trophy,
one of aviation's highest awards.

112
00:06:21,980 --> 00:06:23,270
The Pogo worked.

113
00:06:23,710 --> 00:06:26,490
But it took a pilot of
Coleman's skill to land it.

114
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:30,060
And remember the rival I
mentioned at the start?

115
00:06:30,710 --> 00:06:33,630
Its story explains why
the Navy got nervous.

116
00:06:35,970 --> 00:06:36,970
Lockheed's XFV-1 never got that far.

117
00:06:37,710 --> 00:06:42,700
It was waiting for a more powerful engine,
the Allison T54, that never materialised.

118
00:06:44,140 --> 00:06:48,400
So it fitted temporary landing gear and
flew its prototype off a runway like a

119
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:49,110
normal plane.

120
00:06:49,990 --> 00:06:54,150
High in the air, its pilot did tip
it up and hang it on its propellers.

121
00:06:54,570 --> 00:06:58,840
But the XFV-1 never once took
off or landed vertically.

122
00:06:59,270 --> 00:07:02,410
So only one tail-sitter
fighter did the full cycle.

123
00:07:02,910 --> 00:07:06,340
And that one showed the Navy
exactly where the problem was.

124
00:07:06,970 --> 00:07:09,450
Think about where this
fighter was meant to live.

125
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:12,120
Not a calm airfield in California.

126
00:07:12,590 --> 00:07:18,320
A small platform on a ship, rolling in
the Atlantic, at night, in bad weather.

127
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:23,560
Flown by an ordinary squadron pilot, not
one of the best test pilots in the country.

128
00:07:24,430 --> 00:07:27,090
Landing backwards, onto
a deck that's moving.

129
00:07:28,180 --> 00:07:29,180
The testing made it clear.

130
00:07:29,920 --> 00:07:34,586
Landing the Pogo demanded exceptional
skill, and that made it unsuitable for

131
00:07:34,586 --> 00:07:37,410
ordinary pilots operating
from ordinary ships.

132
00:07:38,110 --> 00:07:39,470
And it wasn't the only problem.

133
00:07:40,030 --> 00:07:44,660
New jet fighters were arriving that were
faster than any propeller-driven fighter,

134
00:07:44,660 --> 00:07:45,450
Pogo included.

135
00:07:45,990 --> 00:07:50,360
And the Pogo's huge gearbox was wearing
out, shedding metal into its oil.

136
00:07:50,910 --> 00:07:57,211
By 1955, the programme was being wound
down, and by 1956, the Navy had ended its

137
00:07:57,211 --> 00:07:58,550
work on the Pogo.

138
00:07:59,870 --> 00:08:00,870
Convair built three Pogo airframes.

139
00:08:01,860 --> 00:08:02,860
Only one ever flew.

140
00:08:03,750 --> 00:08:08,074
That aircraft still exists in the collection
of the Smithsonian's National Air and

141
00:08:08,074 --> 00:08:08,760
Space Museum.

142
00:08:09,340 --> 00:08:11,000
But the idea didn't die.

143
00:08:11,460 --> 00:08:15,290
In the 1960s, the Harrier took a
different route to vertical flight.

144
00:08:15,770 --> 00:08:19,950
It kept the pilot level and pointed
the engine's thrust down instead.

145
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:24,600
The V-22 Osprey tilts its
rotors, not the whole aircraft.

146
00:08:25,050 --> 00:08:29,650
And some modern drones do take off and
land on their tails, just like the Pogo,

147
00:08:29,650 --> 00:08:32,620
because there's nobody
inside who has to look back.

148
00:08:33,230 --> 00:08:36,140
Every answer that came after
has one thing in common.

149
00:08:36,670 --> 00:08:39,610
The pilot never has to
land looking backwards.

150
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:43,520
The Pogo proved a fighter could
take off without a runway.

151
00:08:43,960 --> 00:08:47,850
It couldn't prove that ordinary
pilots could land it safely, at sea.

152
00:08:48,910 --> 00:08:53,027
If you were in that seat, looking back over
your shoulder at the ground, would you

153
00:08:53,027 --> 00:08:53,830
have trusted it?

154
00:08:54,370 --> 00:08:55,400
Tell me in the comments.

155
00:08:56,020 --> 00:09:00,631
And the Pogo wasn't even the strangest
answer to "how do you fight without a

156
00:09:00,631 --> 00:09:05,666
runway?" A few years earlier, the Air Force
tried a fighter with no landing gear at

157
00:09:05,666 --> 00:09:07,850
all, one that lived inside a bomber.

158
00:09:08,340 --> 00:09:09,980
That story is right here.

159
00:09:10,210 --> 00:09:15,117
And if you want one strange aircraft
explained every week, subscribe to Hangar

160
00:09:15,117 --> 00:09:15,620
Physics.

